  "question": {
    "text": "<p>Let&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi><mfenced><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt></mrow><msqrt><mn>7</mn></msqrt></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mfrac><mn>6</mn><msqrt><mn>7</mn></msqrt></mfrac></mrow></mfenced><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>R</mi></math>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>S</mi></math>&nbsp;be four points on the&nbsp;ellipse&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>9</mn><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>36</mn></math>.&nbsp;Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi><mi>Q</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi><mi>S</mi></math> be mutually perpendicular and pass through the origin. If&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mfenced><mrow><mi>P</mi><mi>Q</mi></mrow></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\"><msup><mfenced><mrow><mi>R</mi><mi>S</mi></mrow></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msup></mstyle></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>p</mi><mi>q</mi></mfrac><mo>,</mo></math>&nbsp;where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>p</mi></math>&nbsp;and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>q</mi></math> are coprime, then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></math>&nbsp;is equal to</p>"
  },
